.SH NAME
nice \- set the priority of a command
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nice
.I [ number ]
.I command
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Nice
without an argument, increments the
.I nice
value for this shell by seven.
With a number argument, 
.B nice 
increments the
.I nice
number of the current shell 
by the given
.I number
(e.g., 'nice -+8' and 'nice -8' 
have the same effect, that is , incrementing the priority by 8).
.PP
With a command argument, \fInice\fR increments the
.I nice
value of the given command by
.I number
and by seven if no number is given.
.PP
The super-user can specify negative niceness 
by using the "nice \--number ..." command.
The command is always executed in a subshell, and the restrictions
placed on commands in simple
.B if
statements apply.
.SH SEE ALSO
nice(1), nohup(1C). 
.br
\fIThe C Shell\fR in the ZEUS Utilities Manual
